WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:05.809
As we mentioned before, Unreal Engine will just package everything by default.

2
00:00:05.809 --> 00:00:10.570
So if packaging size is really an issue for you, say you're shipping on mobile

3
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:14.609
or a certain console that really needs a specific file size, what,

4
00:00:14.609 --> 00:00:17.609
what kind of things can we do to help reduce the package size here?

5
00:00:17.609 --> 00:00:19.210
So got 10 tips.

6
00:00:19.649 --> 00:00:23.250
I just want to note right on the outset that we don't have to follow

7
00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:28.730
any or all of these tips, but if packaging size is a focus for you,

8
00:00:28.969 --> 00:00:31.969
then some or all of them might be worth exploring.

9
00:00:31.969 --> 00:00:33.090
So let's just dig into them.

10
00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:35.450
You can start with an empty project.

11
00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:39.210
So as I've been mentioning, you know, where we're starting with maybe the third

12
00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.689
person template or the first person template, you can just start with an empty

13
00:00:43.689 --> 00:00:45.729
project that comes with nothing.

14
00:00:46.049 --> 00:00:48.009
And that will be the cleanest version.

15
00:00:48.009 --> 00:00:51.570
You know, you won't have the starter content, for example, and, you know,

16
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.049
starting with an empty projects is a good first step.

17
00:00:55.530 --> 00:01:01.289
Also as a project set in, there is an option to create compressed cooked packages.

18
00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:05.129
Now I wouldn't, I wouldn't take this out of the box.

19
00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:10.129
This is again, a heavy handed approach, but it will really try and compress

20
00:01:10.129 --> 00:01:14.650
and squeeze down the most out of your cooked content that it can.

21
00:01:15.519 --> 00:01:18.920
It's not needed for everyone, but again, if you're really, really looking to

22
00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.959
squeeze out every megabyte here, you can look at the compressed packages.

23
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.879
You can also exclude editor content when cooking.

24
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:35.219
So the editor comes with, you know, cubes and skydomes and HDRI textures

25
00:01:35.219 --> 00:01:39.500
and all these kind of great useful things out of the box that

26
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.019
help when you're building levels.

27
00:01:41.819 --> 00:01:44.780
However, you could make a copy of some of them.

28
00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:48.540
So you could copy the engine content over to your project,

29
00:01:48.579 --> 00:01:54.209
your games content folder, and just make sure again, redirects are fixed

30
00:01:54.209 --> 00:01:59.329
and all that good stuff, but if you do want to exclude the editor content,

31
00:01:59.329 --> 00:02:03.409
again, this setting is found in project settings, you need to ensure that

32
00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:05.049
you're not using that editor content.

33
00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:09.050
Cause it will, you know, if you get a skydome that disappears or cubes

34
00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.050
that disappear for some reason, you know, that's because you've

35
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.729
excluded the engine content when cooking.

36
00:02:13.729 --> 00:02:17.210
So it can be a way to reduce the file size, but again, just make sure

37
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.770
you're not using that editor content.

38
00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.569
Just to reiterate from a previous slide, development builds

39
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.210
are larger than shipping builds.

40
00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.090
So if you're just deploying the development builds and thinking,

41
00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.009
oh, why is our file size not going down?

42
00:02:29.289 --> 00:02:30.889
Always just check that shipping build.

43
00:02:30.889 --> 00:02:35.289
Cause that'll be your best bet at the file size of what your actual

44
00:02:35.289 --> 00:02:38.650
build will be when you deploy the build, you can make sure you

45
00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.250
cook only the maps that you need.

46
00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:45.129
As I mentioned at the start, you can use a size map on your assets to determine

47
00:02:45.129 --> 00:02:48.050
if anything is there that shouldn't be.

48
00:02:48.129 --> 00:02:53.330
So say we had a blueprint and we use a size map, I've linked the URL

49
00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:54.810
for how to use a size map here.

50
00:02:55.169 --> 00:02:59.930
But if we use a size map on like a blueprint, for example, you can see

51
00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:06.129
that maybe there's a skeletal mesh attached to the blueprint and maybe

52
00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:10.689
that skeletal mesh is just a small skeletal mesh, but it's using 10 4k

53
00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:13.810
textures and you're like, oh wow, I didn't know they were all in the blueprint.

54
00:03:14.009 --> 00:03:16.689
And you just want to go through and like optimize some of the content.

55
00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:22.689
So size map can be great for finding oversized content that

56
00:03:22.689 --> 00:03:24.689
is unnecessary for your project.

57
00:03:25.639 --> 00:03:28.560
Like I mentioned previously, we can migrate maps into a new project.

58
00:03:29.159 --> 00:03:32.560
We can go through and remove any unnecessary plugins.

59
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:40.439
So your plugin window can be found under the edit bar and then plugins

60
00:03:40.479 --> 00:03:42.120
next to where your project settings are.

61
00:03:43.439 --> 00:03:46.919
I wouldn't just go through and untick all plugins because you may not know

62
00:03:46.919 --> 00:03:50.560
what each plugin does and some plugins are needed for how you're

63
00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:51.919
developing in Unreal Engine.

64
00:03:52.319 --> 00:03:56.159
But if you know there's a plugin there that is unnecessary and it shouldn't

65
00:03:56.159 --> 00:04:00.719
be enabled, just go through and remove it again, tread carefully, make sure

66
00:04:00.719 --> 00:04:02.400
you know what that plugin is doing.

67
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.599
If you have to Google what the plugin is, do that, but removing unnecessary

68
00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:08.240
plugins can help reduce file size.

69
00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:14.379
There is a noodle texture plugin that is great for texture compression,

70
00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:15.939
which you can look into.

71
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:20.259
And nano is a tiny project template for people who want to make

72
00:04:20.939 --> 00:04:22.899
tiny games, UE4 and UE5.

73
00:04:22.899 --> 00:04:26.180
So, you know, if you're really, if this is a passion project of yours and

74
00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.259
you really want to squeeze out every megabyte, maybe you want to check

75
00:04:29.259 --> 00:04:32.420
out the nano project there.
